@title What Eighteen Months of Four-Day Weeks Did to Our Support Team
@subtitle Halden Cartography blog
@author Priscilla Adeyemi-Koh, Head of Customer Support
@date 2026-10-18

## Why we tried it

I run a support team of 23 people, and in 2024 I watched five of them leave. Our annual attrition was 21%, and when we read the exit conversations, one reason came up more than any other: burnout. Not pay, not the product, not the customers. People were simply tired in a way that weekends no longer fixed.

I did not go looking for a four-day week as a perk. I went looking for a way to keep good people, and it happened to be the one idea nobody on the team could stop talking about. So we agreed to try it as a pilot, with a clear start and a clear way out. The pilot began on 2025-04-07. It ended on 2026-10-02, when we decided to make it permanent.

I was curious and, honestly, a little nervous. Support is the one function where "we are closed on Fridays" can sound like a broken promise to a customer.

## How the schedule works

Everyone works Monday to Thursday, 9 hours a day. That is 36 hours a week at full pay, with no cut to salary.

Friday is the hard part, and the answer is a rotating on-call group of 4 people. They cover Friday, and in return they take Monday off instead. Each person rotates in and out of that group, so nobody ends up owning Fridays forever. The on-call group handles the queue, escalations and anything urgent, and everything else waits until Monday.

It sounds tidy written down. In practice the first few weeks were full of small corrections, and I will get to those.

## What happened to the numbers

I expected some metric to get worse, because that is the usual price of working less. Instead, nearly everything moved the right way.

- Median first response time fell from 3.4 hours to 2.9 hours.
- Customer satisfaction rose from 4.3 to 4.5 out of 5.
- Annual attrition fell from 21% to 9%.
- Sick days fell by 31%.

The attrition number is the one I care about most, because it is the reason we started. Going from 21% to 9% means the team I have today knows the product, knows the customers and knows each other. That experience shows up everywhere else, including the faster responses.

I do not want to oversell this. We changed other things during those eighteen months, and I cannot prove the schedule caused every improvement. But the direction was consistent across measures that have no business moving together unless something real was happening.

## What got harder

Handoffs on Thursday evening were the first problem. When the whole team stops at the same time, anything half finished has to be written up properly, or it sits over the long weekend and a customer waits three days for an answer. We had to get much better at notes, and we are still not perfect.

The second problem was two enterprise customers who wanted guaranteed Friday phone support. A rotating on-call group was not enough for what they were paying for, and they were right to say so. We hired 2 more people to cover it. That cost money, and I think it is worth saying plainly that the schedule was not free.

The third was smaller but real. A few people found 9-hour days tiring in winter, when it is dark at both ends of the day. For them, a longer day and a shorter week was a trade that only half worked. We are still talking about how to help, including flexible start times.

## If you try it

Here is what I would tell another team lead.

First, pilot for at least 6 months. A month tells you about novelty. Six months tells you about winter, about a busy season, and about whether the early gains hold up.

Second, write the coverage rules before the first week. Who is on call, what counts as urgent, what happens when someone is sick on a Friday. We wrote ours in the middle of the pilot and I wish we had done it earlier.

Third, measure customer metrics alongside team morale. It is easy to feel that everything is going well because the team is happier. Response time and satisfaction told us whether customers agreed, and they did. If they had not, we would have wanted to know early.

I started this because I was worried about my team. Eighteen months later I am less worried, and I have the numbers to explain why.
